  [L1] Ambiguity detection: 5 issue(s) found
    1. kpi_targets.value_per_resource / assumptions.resource_scope
       Q: Is there a hard cap on any individual resource dimension (e.g. total energy kWh, raw-material kg, or overtime labor hours) for this shift? Without one, the optimiser may increase absolute energy consumption by ~40% or more if that marginally improves the value-per-resource ratio — substituting cheap resources for expensive ones in ways you did not intend.
       A: Cap each resource dimension at 110% of its historical shift average (baseline × 1.10); the optimiser may not exceed this ceiling on any single resource even if the aggregate ratio improves.
    2. kpi_targets.value_per_resource / assumptions.value_scope
       Q: Should delivered value be measured as throughput × unit margin (volume-focused), or should a quality multiplier — such as first-pass yield or defect-rate penalty — scale the output value? The two formulations can send the optimiser in opposite directions: one rewards speed, the other rewards precision.
       A: Value = throughput × per-unit margin with no quality weight; quality defects are treated as waste that inflates the resource denominator, not as a direct value penalty.
    3. hard_constraints.noise_level / assumptions.noise_measurement
       Q: Does the 80 dB limit apply to the shift-averaged LAeq, or must instantaneous peak levels also stay below 80 dB? A 90 dB burst lasting 15 minutes can average below 80 dB LAeq over an 8-hour shift yet still pose a hearing-damage risk to operators working near the source.
       A: Enforce 80 dB as the LAeq limit and add an unmonitored instantaneous ceiling of 85 dB (ISO 9612 short-term exposure limit); any solution where estimated peak noise exceeds 85 dB is treated as infeasible.
    4. hard_constraints.operator_fatigue / assumptions.fatigue_measurement
       Q: At what frequency is the fatigue index sampled, and must fatigue remain ≤ 0.4 at every sample point or only as a shift-level average? If sampled hourly, the optimiser could legally schedule a 45-minute high-intensity burst with fatigue peaking at 0.65 between readings while the hourly reading stays at 0.38.
       A: Enforce fatigue ≤ 0.4 at every 15-minute interval; solutions that require sustained fatigue above 0.4 between any two consecutive 15-minute checkpoints are treated as infeasible.
    5. context.scope / kpi_targets (missing minimum throughput floor)
       Q: Is there a minimum production quota — in units, revenue, or shift utilisation percentage — that must be achieved regardless of efficiency gains? Without a production floor, the optimiser can satisfy all hard constraints and still maximise value-per-resource by running a very small, slow job that leaves most capacity idle.
       A: No explicit minimum throughput is enforced; the optimiser may produce any feasible volume. If shift utilisation drops below 60% of rated capacity, a warning flag is raised for manager review but the solution remains valid.

