    1. priority_order vs _raw_priority_order
       Q: Should human wellbeing (fatigue, noise) act only as hard constraints that block infeasible schedules, or should the optimiser also actively minimise fatigue and noise below their limits even at the cost of lower value_efficiency — and if so, what is the acceptable efficiency sacrifice (e.g. up to 10% loss in value_efficiency to reduce fatigue from 0.4 to 0.3)?
       A: Treat fatigue and noise purely as hard constraints (reject any schedule that violates them); maximise value_efficiency freely within the feasible region. The 'HumanWellbeing' priority label does not further penalise schedules that approach but do not cross the limits.
    2. value_efficiency (maximize, threshold: null)
       Q: Is there a saturation point above which further efficiency gains are not worth the operational risk — for example, should the optimiser stop pushing once value_efficiency exceeds a target such as 1.25× the shift baseline, rather than driving the system to its theoretical maximum?
       A: No efficiency ceiling is applied; the optimiser treats value_efficiency as unbounded-maximize and will exploit every feasible margin up to the fatigue and noise hard limits. If this causes resource use to rise more than 40% above baseline, a human review flag is emitted but the schedule is not rejected.
    3. context.temporal_horizon (shift_duration unspecified)
       Q: How many hours does this shift last (e.g. 8 h, 10 h, or 12 h)? A 12-hour shift with a fatigue limit of 0.4 implies a maximum sustainable cycle-rate roughly 25% lower than the same limit on an 8-hour shift, which can make high-throughput schedules infeasible.
       A: Assume a standard 8-hour shift. If actual shift length differs, the fatigue accumulation model will be miscalibrated and the optimiser may generate schedules that violate the 0.4 limit in practice.
    4. assumptions.resource_scope (labor_energy_material weighting)
       Q: How should labor, energy, and material inputs be weighted when computing the single 'resource_unit' denominator of value_efficiency — for instance, if a faster cycle reduces material waste by 5% but increases energy draw by 15%, does that count as a net resource saving or a net resource cost?
       A: All three resource types are converted to a common monetary cost using current standard rates (labor $/h, energy $/kWh, material $/unit), then summed. The optimiser maximises delivered-value divided by total-cost. If cost data are unavailable, equal unit weights are applied and a data-gap warning is logged.
    5. noise_level (assumptions.noise_measurement — Leq vs instantaneous)
       Q: Does the 80 dB limit apply as a continuous equivalent level (Leq) averaged over the shift, or as a hard instantaneous ceiling that must never be exceeded at any moment — because many standard machine operations produce brief spikes above 80 dB A that are acceptable under time-averaged occupational standards but would violate an instantaneous cap?
       A: Apply the limit as a 15-minute rolling Leq (A-weighted), consistent with ISO 9612 occupational noise assessment. Instantaneous transients up to 85 dB lasting fewer than 30 seconds are permitted provided the rolling average remains at or below 80 dB.

